          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %strong 2-topos
            is the generalization of the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-categories"} 2-categories
          %p
            There are multiple conceivable such generalizations, depending in particular on whether one tries to generalize the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
            or of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+topos"} elementary topos
          %p
            In contrast,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2C1%29-topos"} (2,1)-toposes
            are much better understood.
          %p
            A
            %em Grothendieck 2-topos
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-sheaves"} 2-sheaves
            over a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-site"} 2-site
          %p
            A
            %em Grothendieck (2,1)-topos
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-category"} (2,1)-category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-sheaves"} (2,1)-sheaves
            over a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-site"} (2,1)-site
          %p
            See also
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+topos+theory"} higher topos theory
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#characterization_of_2sheaf_2toposes Characterization of 2-sheaf 2-toposes
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-toposes"} 2-toposes
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-sheaves"} 2-sheaves
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-site"} 2-site
            are special among all 2-toposes, in direct generalization of how
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+toposes"} sheaf toposes
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+toposes"}> Grothendieck toposes
            ”) are special among all
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giraud%27s+theorem"} Giraud's theorem
            famously characterizes sheaf toposes. This characterization has a 2-categorical analog: the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-Giraud+theorem"} 2-Giraud theorem
          %h3#localic_2toposes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n,r)
            \-Localic 2-toposes
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-sheaf"} 2-sheaf
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-topos"} 2-topos
            is “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n,r)
            \-localic” or “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n,r)
            \-truncated” if it has an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2Cr%29-site"} (n,r)-site
            of definition.
          %p
            In particular a
            = succeed "-localic" do
